{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was shaped by the turbulent politics of the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Because of his young age a regency was established led by his mother and later by the King of Jerusalem. His minority lasted several years during which Tripoli faced internal unrest and external threats from Muslim forces. Raymond eventually assumed full control of his county and began to assert his authority over the nobility and the military orders.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures and later to Agnes of Courtenay who was the mother of King Baldwin IV. His connection to the royal family gave him great influence. Raymond was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 when Baldwin IV became too ill to rule. He was a skilled diplomat and often tried to negotiate peace with Saladin. His cautious approach contrasted with the aggressive policies of other nobles such as Guy of Lusignan. Raymond believed that diplomacy and alliances were more sustainable than constant warfare. His efforts to avoid conflict were sometimes seen as weakness but they reflected a deep understanding of the fragile state of the Crusader territories.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who opposed the decision to march to Tiberias in the face of Saladin's siege. He warned that the army would be exposed to dehydration and ambush. His advice was ignored and the Crusader army was destroyed at Hattin. Raymond himself was captured during the battle and later released. He returned to Tripoli where he died in 1187. His death marked the end of a long and complex political career. Raymond III was remembered as a prudent and intelligent leader who tried to preserve the Crusader states through careful strategy rather than reckless bravery. His legacy is one of diplomacy in a time of war and of a man who understood the limits of his power.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-0